WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.530
Subsurface scattering means that not every
ray of light is reflected or absorbed on the

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.780
surface of a material, but some of it may
get inside somewhere, and come out somewhere

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:15.780
else.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.650
For instance, our skin is a great and fairly
unknown example of that.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:25.080
We can witness this beautiful effect if we
place a strong light source behind our ears.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.490
Note that many other materials, such as plant
leaves, many fruits such as apples and oranges,

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:35.350
wax, marble also have subsurface scattering.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:40.489
The more we look at objects like these, the
more we recognize how beautiful and ubiquitous

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:44.780
subsurface scattering and translucency is
in mother nature.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.190
And today, our main question is whether we
can reproduce this kind of effect with 3D

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.320
printed materials.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.100
The input would be a real material, such as
these slabs, and the output would be an arbitrary

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.010
shaped 3d printed material with similar scattering
properties.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.520
Something that looks similar.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.759
What you see here is already the result of
the 3D printing process, and wow, they look

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:13.210
very tasty indeed.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:18.659
The process starts with a measurement apparatus
where we grab a real material, and create

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:24.470
a diffusion profile from it that describes
how light scatters inside of this material.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.759
We have talked quite a bit about diffusion
profiles before, I've put some links to earlier

00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:31.060
episodes in the video description box.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:36.130
If you check it out, you'll see how we can
add subsurface scattering to an already existing

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.070
image by "kind of" multiplying it with an
other image.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.439
This is another one of those amazing inventions
of mankind.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:45.950
Now, onto 3D printing.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.799
When we would like to 3d print something,
we basically have a few different materials

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:54.890
to work with, and we have to specify a shape.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.460
This shape is approximated with a three-dimensional
grid.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.240
Each of these tiny grid elements typically
have the thickness of several microns, which

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.920
basically means a tiny fraction of the diameter
of one hair strand, and we like to call these

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.300
elements voxels.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.970
Now, before printing, we have to specify what
kind of material we'd like to fill each of

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.450
these voxels with.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:21.110
This is the general workflow for most 3D printers.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.570
What is specific to this work is that, after
that, we have to take one column of this material,

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.940
and look at the scattering properties of it.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.470
Let's call this column one stacking.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:36.840
We could measure that stacking by hand and
see how it relates to the original target

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.050
material, and we are trying to minimize the
difference between the two.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:45.480
However, it would take millions of tries and
would likely take a lifetime to print just

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.860
one high-quality reproduction.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.560
So basically, we have an optimization problem
where we're looking for a stacking that will

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.920
appear similar to the chosen diffusion profiles.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.690
The difference between the appearance of the
two is to be minimized.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:05.850
However, we have to realize, that in physics,
the laws of light scattering are well understood,

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:10.620
and the wonderful thing is that instead of
printing a real object, we could just use

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:15.030
a light simulation program to tell us how
close the results should be.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:20.230
Now, this would work great, but it would still
take an eternity because simulating light

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:25.690
scattering through a stack of materials would
take the very least, several seconds.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:30.730
And we have to try up to millions of stackings
for each column, and there is a lot of columns

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:31.730
to compute.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.570
Why a lot of different columns?

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:38.370
Well, it's because we have a heterogeneous
problem, which means that the whole material

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.610
can contain variations in color and scattering
properties.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.870
The geometry may also be uneven, so this is
a vastly more difficult formulation of the

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:48.870
initial problem.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:54.260
A classical light simulation program would
be able to solve this, well, in a matter of

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.260
years.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.500
However, there is a wonderful tool that is
able to almost immediately tell us how much

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.870
light is scattering inside of a stack of a
ton of different materials.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.480
An almost instant multi-layer scattering tool,
if you will.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.750
It really is a miracle that we can get the
results for something so quickly that would

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:19.340
otherwise require following the paths of millions
of light rays.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.950
We call this technique the Hankel transform.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:27.100
The mathematical description of it is absolutely
beautiful, but I personally think the best

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.910
way of motivating these techniques is through
application.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:31.910
Like this one.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:37.000
Imagine that many mathematicians have to study
this transform without ever hearing what it

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.670
can be used for.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:44.090
These are not some dry and tedious materials
that one has to memorize - we can do miracles

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:47.680
with these inventions, and I feel that people
need to know about that!

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.590
With the use of the Hankel transform and some
additional optimizations, one can efficiently

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:57.400
find solutions that lead to high-quality reproductions
of the input material.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:02.160
Excellent piece of work, definitely one of
my favorites in 3D fabrication.

